The bug is that the function checks for `null`, `undefined`, and `false` explicitly, but fails to check for empty strings (`""`). In JavaScript, an empty string is a "falsy" value, but it is not strictly equal to `null`, `undefined`, or `false`. Therefore, the condition `input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false` evaluates to `true` for an empty string, causing the function to return `true`.

To fix this, you should also check if the input is an empty string. Alternatively, since the goal seems to be rejecting falsy values (empty strings, null, undefined, false), you could simply rely on truthiness, but given the explicit checks, adding a check for empty string is the most direct fix that preserves the existing logic style.

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// validateInput("")         -> false
// validateInput(null)       -> false
// validateInput(undefined)  -> false
// validateInput("hello")    -> true
</solution>

