The code has two main bugs:

1.  **Incorrect Comparison Logic**: The condition `if data[i] == max_val` compares the current element against the *global* maximum value found so far, rather than the *current* streak's value. This means it only increments `current_count` if the current element matches the best streak seen so far, ignoring other potential streaks. It should compare `data[i]` with `data[i-1]` to detect if the current streak continues.
2.  **Missing Final Check**: After the loop finishes, the last streak is not compared against the global maximum. If the longest streak ends at the last element of the array, it will be ignored.

Here is the corrected code:

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    if data.is_empty() {
        return (0, 0);
    }

    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            // Streak broken, check if previous streak was the longest
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = data[i - 1];
            }
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    // Check the last streak after the loop finishes
    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

