The bug is a classic closure variable capture issue. In Go (prior to version 1.22), the loop variable `i` is shared across all iterations of the loop. When you create a goroutine with `go func() { ... }()`, it captures the *variable* `i` by reference, not its value at the time of creation.

By the time the goroutines actually execute and print `i`, the loop has likely already finished, leaving `i` with its final value (5). All five goroutines see this same shared variable, hence the output `5 5 5 5 5`.

To fix this, you need to pass the current value of `i` as an argument to the anonymous function. This creates a new local copy of the value for each goroutine.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

