The code has two main bugs:

1.  **Incorrect comparison logic**: The condition `if data[i] == max_val` checks if the current element matches the *global* maximum value found so far, rather than checking if it continues the *current* streak. This means once a new potential max is identified, subsequent elements are only counted if they match that specific global max, ignoring other values entirely. It should check against the previous element (`data[i-1]`) to determine if the streak continues.

2.  **Missing final check**: After the loop finishes, the count of the last streak (`current_count`) is never compared against `max_count`. If the longest streak occurs at the end of the array, it will be ignored unless it was already larger than the previous max during an intermediate step (which might not happen correctly due to bug #1). We need to perform one last check after the loop.

Here is the corrected code:

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    if data.is_empty() {
        return (0, 0);
    }

    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        // Check if current element continues the streak from the previous element
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            // Streak broken. Check if the previous streak was the longest.
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = data[i - 1];
            }
            // Reset current streak counter for the new value
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    // Final check for the last streak which ends at the end of the array
    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

