The bug is caused by **slice aliasing**.

When you initialize `positive` and `negative` using `nums[:0]`, both slices share the same underlying array as `nums`. When you append to `positive`, it writes elements into this shared underlying array starting at index 0. When you subsequently append to `negative`, it also starts writing at index 0 of the *same* underlying array, overwriting the data previously written by `positive`.

To fix this, you must create independent backing arrays for each result slice. The standard way to do this in Go is to use `make([]int, 0)` or `[]int{}` instead of slicing the input.

