The error `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')` indicates that you are trying to access the `.city` property on an object that is `undefined`.

In the line:
```javascript
const city = order.shipping_address.city;
```

JavaScript evaluates this from left to right. It first accesses `order`, then `order.shipping_address`. If `order.shipping_address` is `undefined` (or null), attempting to access `.city` on it throws the TypeError.

This happens in production likely because some orders do not have a `shipping_address` field populated, whereas your test data ("works on my machine") always had valid addresses.

To fix this, you should use **Optional Chaining** (`?.`) to safely access nested properties. This will return `undefined` instead of throwing an error if any part of the chain is missing. You can also provide a default value for `city` to ensure the logic remains robust.

Here is the corrected code using optional chaining and a fallback for the city name:

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function getShippingZone(order) {
    const city = order?.shipping_address?.city || "";
    const zones = {
        "New York": "east",
        "Los Angeles": "west",
        "Chicago": "central",
    };
    return zones[city] || "standard";
}
</solution>

