To find the 10 largest files under `/workspace/data/` and format them with human-readable sizes (like `du -h`) sorted by descending size, we can use `find` to list all files with their exact byte sizes, then process this data using `sort` and `awk`.

1.  **Find files**: `find /workspace/data/ -type f -printf '%s\t%p\n'` lists all regular files (`-type f`) followed by their size in bytes (`%s`), a tab, and the path (`%p`).
2.  **Sort by size**: `sort -nr -k1,1` sorts the output numerically (`-n`) in reverse order (`-r`) based on the first column (`-k1,1`, which is the byte size).
3.  **Format sizes**: `awk` reads each line, takes the first field (size in bytes) and converts it to a human-readable string using logic similar to `du -h` (dividing by 1024 and appending K, M, G, etc.). It prints the formatted size, a tab, and the original path.
4.  **Limit results**: `head -n 10` ensures only the top 10 lines are kept.
5.  **Write to file**: The final output is redirected to `/workspace/top10.txt`.

The command sequence below implements this pipeline.

<solution verdict="run">
find /workspace/data/ -type f -printf '%s\t%p\n' | sort -nr -k1,1 | head -n 10 | awk '{
    size = $1;
    if (size >= 1073741824) {
        printf "%.1fG\t%s\n", size / 1073741824, substr($0, index($0, "\t") + 1);
    } else if (size >= 1048576) {
        printf "%.1fM\t%s\n", size / 1048576, substr($0, index($0, "\t") + 1);
    } else if (size >= 1024) {
        printf "%.1fK\t%s\n", size / 1024, substr($0, index($0, "\t") + 1);
    } else {
        printf "%d\t%s\n", size, substr($0, index($0, "\t") + 1);
    }
}' > /workspace/top10.txt
</solution>

